<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blaise Pascal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1623">1623</a>–<a href="/years/1662">1662</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Blaise Pascal was a French mathematician, physicist, inventor, philosopher and Catholic writer. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont-Ferrand</a> and taught by his father Étienne Pascal, he wrote a treatise on conic sections at the age of 16 and from 1642 worked on calculating machines, later called Pascalines. He clarified the concepts of pressure and vacuum, generalising the work of Evangelista Torricelli, and his correspondence with Pierre de Fermat in 1654 gave rise to the mathematical theory of probability. After a religious experience in late 1654 he wrote the Lettres provinciales, on the conflict between Jansenists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, and the Pensées, which contains Pascal's wager. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1662, aged 39.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blaise-pascal.jpg" alt="Blaise Pascal" width="330" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown; a copy of the painting of François II Quesnel, which was made for Gérard Edelinck en 1691 [réf. nécessaire]. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blaise_Pascal_Versailles,_circa_1690.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Evangelista Torricelli’s barometer of 1643 and the experiment on the Puy de Dôme that <b>Blaise Pascal</b> arranged in 1648 showed that the air has weight, pressing less on a mountain top than at its foot, and Boyle, whose assistant Robert Hooke built him an air pump to study…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have discovered that all the unhappiness of men arises from one single fact, that they cannot stay quietly in their own chamber.&#8221;</p><footer>Pensées, section 2 &#183; tr. W. F. Trotter, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Blaise_Pascal/Thoughts/Section_2" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Cleopatra's nose: had it been shorter, the whole aspect of the world would have been altered.&#8221;</p><footer>Pensées, section 2 &#183; tr. W. F. Trotter, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Blaise_Pascal/Thoughts/Section_2" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The eternal silence of these infinite spaces frightens me.&#8221;</p><footer>Pensées, section 3 &#183; tr. W. F. Trotter, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Blaise_Pascal/Thoughts/Section_3" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
